ll M} g + mean_{lab} h = mean_all Y + [mean_lab h − mean_all h]. Hence\n- **V(g) = Var(Y)/M + (1/n − 1/M)·Var(h)** (the two terms are uncorrelated: the first is fixed given the M units, the second is a mean-zero simple-random-sampling error). Minimised by g* = m(Z) = E[Y|Z]:\n  **floor V* = σ²_res/n + V_g/M**, σ²_res = E Var(Y|Z), V_g = Var m(Z). For any fixed g, Var(h) = σ²_res + E(m−g)², so\n  **excess over the floor = (1/n − 1/M)·E(m − g)², exactly** (Pythagoras). \"Regular estimators cannot do better than V*\" is the classical MCAR semiparametric-efficiency statement\n  (Robins, Rotnitzky & Zhao, JASA 1994; Tsiatis, *Semiparametric Theory and Missing Data*, 2006 — cited for that half; not read in full by this role; the paper either cites it or gives a short proof).\n- **PPI++ derived exactly** (scalar λ, g = λf, unlabeled-only averaging): V_{PPI++}(λ) = Var(Y−λf)/n + λ²Var(f)/N, minimised at λ* = Cov(Y,f)/[Var(f)(1+n/N)],\n  value **V_{PPI++} = (Var(Y)/n)(1 − ρ²/(1+n/N))**, ρ = corr(Y,f). The pooled estimator with the best linear g = a*+b*f has *the same* variance (Var(h)=Var(Y)(1−ρ²) gives Var(Y)/n·[1 − ρ²(1 − n/M)], and 1−n/M = 1/(1+n/N)):\n  PPI++ with tuned λ is the pooled linear estimator, with λ = b*·N/M. **Consequence: with λ tuned, the \"pooled Z\" step is already inside PPI++ and is worth exactly zero for linear g.**\n  The free gain V_g(1/N − 1/M) belongs to λ=1 PPI (and to any mis-scaled g), i.e. to the PPI → PPI++ step. Exact excess of PPI++ over the floor:\n  **V_{PPI++} − V* = (1/n − 1/M)·Δ, Δ = E(m(f) − a* − b*f)²** (best-linear-approximation gap of E[Y|f]). **No room iff Δ = 0, i.e. E[Y|f] is affine** (Gaussian synthetic case: closed form).\n  The calibrated route is the same family with g = ĝ ≈ m and λ* = N/M (=1/(1+n/N)); it attains V* at ĝ = m.\n- **Break-even n (same units on both sides):** cross-fitting with K folds learns ĝ on n_train = (K−1)n/K labels; its learning cost is an excess E(ĝ−g_lim)² in Var(h) of ≈ d_eff σ²_res/n_train\n  (parametric/spline calibrators, d_eff = effective df; isotonic and trees are non-parametric, excess risk ≈ n^{−2/3} or slower, so d_eff σ²_res/n_train is replaced by c·n_train^{−2/3} for them — cost model stated per calibrator).\n  The gain is Δ in the same quantity; both are multiplied by (1/n − 1/M), which cancels: **break-even n* ≈ d_eff σ²_res/Δ · (K−1)/K** (parametric calibrator). Labels-only\n  **headroom diagnostic**: Δ̂ (cross-fitted) − learning cost, with its own CI. Predicted n* is checked on synthetic Gaussian and sigmoid cases.\n- **Validity and the variance estimator.** SE² = V̂ar(Y)/M + (1/n − 1/M)·V̂ar(h), with V̂ar(Y) the labeled sample variance and h_i = Y_i − ĝ^{(−k(i))}(Z_i) (out-of-fold, pooled across folds; per-fold pooling also reported).\n  The estimate is (1/M)[Σ_unl ḡ(Z) + Σ_lab ĝ^{(−k(i))}(Z_i)] + (1/n)Σ_lab h_i, ḡ = average of the fold models. Because the propensity \n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "eecdd4c9-0842-5bbe-b514-9c0274d6578f", "at": 1795676131.6647809, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 4001, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "a963ffaef850ae11bbae616899e08e70", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "67a21abd-aab8-515c-84c2-62c012fdcd54", "at": 1795676131.7605639, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 4002, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "a963ffaef850ae11bbae616899e08e70", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "6ccc93d0-dba3-5ddf-bf3d-1cbbd2f98716", "at": 1795676131.7791638, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 4003, "la